//! Power rails.

/// A DC power rail: two terminals and the voltage between them. A rail on a board that
/// nothing on the board drives is supplied from outside it.
///
/// `current` is what whatever is behind the rail draws from the net through `pos`: positive
/// when it draws, negative when it supplies. A design states the load side
/// (`.output.current &= 0mA..50mA`); the checker adds up the rails on each net so that
/// what is drawn equals what is supplied (ADR 0007).
///
/// `neg` is on `GND` unless the board says otherwise (`.neg.net &= "AGND"`, ADR 0121).
interface Rail {
    .pos: Pin
    .neg: Pin
    .voltage: Voltage
    .current: Current
    /// The highest voltage the rail reaches, transients included (a clamp's voltage, a load
    /// dump). `voltage` is the steady range. Rails joined with `~` share it, and a part that
    /// limits a transient states it for the rail behind it.
    .peak: Voltage
    /// The highest voltage the rail reaches the wrong way round, as a positive number: the
    /// negative transient of a vehicle's supply, or what a miswired harness puts on it (the
    /// check treats it as a voltage that must be blocked). A design states it for the input,
    /// where it can happen, and the first part of a `~>` chain from the rail that has a
    /// `.supply_reverse_peak` is checked against it (ADR 0091). Left unstated, nothing is
    /// checked and nothing is listed as not evaluated: the member is `optional` (ADR 0074),
    /// because a member that is not would list one more note for every diode or TVS diode in
    /// a chain from any rail, such as the rail behind a diode, which has no reverse peak and
    /// needs none. It is a positive number (a datasheet says `-100V`), so that every
    /// rating reads `<=` in the same direction as `.peak`; the number has the opposite sign to
    /// the voltage it is. Only the first part of the chain that has the field is given it:
    /// the part that meets the reversed supply blocks it or conducts it, and what is behind
    /// is protected by it (a TVS diode behind a blocking diode would be found to conduct a
    /// transient it never sees). A rail behind a blocking diode states `.reverse_peak &= 0V`
    /// itself, since nothing derives it. The reverse voltage a diode sees is the supply's
    /// reverse peak plus whatever voltage the rail behind it holds (a capacitor charged to the
    /// input's voltage), which is not added: a board states `.d.max_reverse_voltage >=
    /// .supply.reverse_peak + .load.peak`.
    .reverse_peak: Voltage optional
    /// The most current the load behind the rail draws at an instant, as a positive number: the
    /// inrush of a capacitor charged through a resistor, a motor's start. `current` is the
    /// steady range, and the DC analysis never reads this one, so it enters no balance, no
    /// power and no shunt window (issue #750). It is the rail's own: two loads joined to one
    /// source each state theirs, and `~` does not share it. Each is checked against
    /// `max_peak_current` alone, without the steady current of the other loads, so a board whose
    /// loads start together states the sum on one of them. Left unstated, nothing is checked and
    /// nothing is listed as not evaluated (ADR 0074).
    .peak_current: Current optional
    /// The most current the source behind the rail gives at an instant, as a positive number.
    /// A regulator states it for its output (its continuous output current, the most the
    /// datasheet promises), and rails joined with `~` share it, as they share `peak`. A load's
    /// `peak_current` must not be above it.
    .max_peak_current: Current optional
    .peak_current <= .max_peak_current
    /// The least voltage the rail may fall to while the regulator behind it is in dropout, below
    /// the least of `.voltage` (issue #1301): an MCU that runs from 1.8 V on a 3.3 V regulator
    /// fed from a falling cell lets the output follow the input down, and the board says how far
    /// (`.v3.voltage_floor &= 2.7V`). `.voltage` stays the range the DC analysis holds the rail
    /// at and the regulator's own checks read; the bounds that speak of the least voltage the
    /// rail reaches are judged over its window, from the floor to the top of `.voltage`. The
    /// dropout check (`.vin.voltage >= min(.vout.voltage) + .dropout`) then needs an input of
    /// the floor plus the dropout, and every load's supply range (`.vcc.voltage >= 2.4V`) must
    /// hold the whole window, or it is an error that names the floor. A value a relation works
    /// out from `.voltage` (a logic level, a resistor's current) is not moved. Rails joined with
    /// `~` share it. Left unstated, the window is `.voltage` and nothing changes (ADR 0074).
    .voltage_floor: Voltage optional
    .voltage_floor <= min(.voltage)
    /// The narrowest track the nets of this rail are routed with: a lower bound, written as
    /// a range (`.min_track &= 0.5mm..`). Both pins get it, since the return current is the
    /// same current. A net takes the widest of the rails on it, so `GND` is as wide as the
    /// widest rail that returns to it (ADR 0020). Unset, the net is routed at the process's
    /// least.
    ///
    /// Why a field of the rail (the owner's decision, 2026-11-02): it adds no construct and
    /// reuses `~`, narrowing and the module interface, since `~` between rails already equates
    /// their fields, so a module's output rail carries the width out with no new rule. Both
    /// ends of a rail get it because the return current is the same current. A width under
    /// the process's least is the least. The router draws the net at the width and keeps clear
    /// of other copper by it; a track leaves a pad at the net's width; the `.kicad_pro` has a
    /// net class for each distinct width (`Track_0.8mm`) with a pattern for each net, with a
    /// `priority`, which KiCad needs to read the class, and a clearance of its own only where a
    /// rail's `.min_clearance` asks for more than the process's least.
    /// Rejected: a class that lists nets (`NetClass { .nets &= "VIN,3V3" }`: it names a net a
    /// second time in a string list, a typo is a silent miss, and a net in two classes needs a
    /// tie-break) and a `net "VIN" { ... }` statement (a second statement shape for what a
    /// rail's field already says for power nets, and another place that spells the net's
    /// name). A signal that must be wide has no rail: widen every net with
    /// `.process.min_track`. A via size per class is not built (issue #60).
    .min_track: Length
    /// The least space between copper of the nets of this rail and copper of any other net: a
    /// lower bound, written as a range (`.min_clearance &= 0.5mm..`), with the rules of
    /// `.min_track` (the owner's decision, 2026-11-15, issue #758). Both pins get it. A net
    /// takes the largest of the rails on it. Unset, or under the process's least
    /// (`.process.min_clearance`), the net keeps the process's least. A module's output rail
    /// carries it out through `~`. Between two nets the larger of their clearances applies, as
    /// KiCad applies it. The router keeps every other net's copper that far from the net's
    /// tracks, vias and pads, the pours keep it, the design rules check judges it, and the
    /// `.kicad_pro` has a net class for each distinct width and clearance (`Clearance_0.5mm`,
    /// `Track_0.8mm_Clearance_0.5mm`) that states it. A high-voltage net, or a rail the board
    /// house wants kept further from other copper, says so here.
    .min_clearance: Length
    // Ground is `GND` unless a rail says otherwise. A board states ground once, by saying
    // nothing: a rail with no statement for `.neg.net` is on `GND`, and a rail with its own
    // ground (isolated, analog) states it, `.agnd_rail: Rail { .neg.net &= "AGND" }`. The
    // name is a default (ADR 0051) and takes effect only where the rail is on a board, since
    // only a board names nets (ADR 0121); a rail joined (`~`) to a pin or rail whose net a
    // `&=` names keeps that name.
    .neg.net ?= "GND"
    /// `"none"`, or what keeps this rail's ground apart from the board's other grounds (`"isolated
    /// DC-DC"`, `"digital isolator"`): an isolated ground. Unset is the same as `"none"`. A rail
    /// that states it has a ground of its own, so it states its net too (`.neg.net &=
    /// "ISO_GND"`), and its ground is refused on the net of a rail of the board that is not
    /// isolated. A system's mates must not join it to another ground of its board, through
    /// other boards' grounds, since that joins the two sides of the barrier. Each ground is a
    /// net of its own, so the voltages, the balance of currents and the current through mated
    /// contacts are read for each apart. Rails joined with `~` share it (issue #793, the
    /// direction of ADR 0040).
    .isolated: String
}

/// The impedance the tracks of one net are held to (issue #1007): one pin that `~` joins to
/// the pin or the net the track is on, and the target from the track to its reference layer. A
/// 50 ohm antenna line is `.rf_line: TrackImpedance { .impedance &= 50ohm }` and
/// `.rf_line.line ~ .rf.signal`. `fab-notes.txt` then says, for each layer a track may be on,
/// the width that gives it, from the process's build-up, and the impedance of the width the
/// router draws. The router draws the net at that width on each layer (issue #1025), never
/// narrower than the process's least track or what the net's current needs; what the board
/// cannot give (no build-up, no width in a formula's domain) is a finding of the guideline
/// `impedance target`.
///
/// Why an interface of its own, joined with `~` (the choice follows `Rail`'s `.min_track`,
/// the owner's decision of 2026-11-02 that a width is a field of a std interface and not a
/// construct): it adds no syntax, a module carries its target out through its interface, and
/// the net is the one the pin is joined to, not a string that a typo would miss. Rejected: a
/// field on `Pin` (every pin of every part would have it) and a `net "RF" { ... }` statement
/// (a second shape for what an interface says, and another place that spells a net's name).
/// The language-design choice here is the smallest the owner's precedent allows, written
/// down for review.
interface TrackImpedance {
    .line: Pin
    /// The impedance, as a single value (`50ohm`).
    .impedance: Resistance
}

/// The impedance between the two tracks of a pair, an edge-coupled differential pair (issue
/// #1007): `.p` and `.n` are joined with `~` to the pair's two nets (`.usb.dp` and
/// `.usb.dm`), `.impedance` is the target between them (90 ohm for USB 2.0) and `.spacing`
/// the space between the two tracks, edge to edge, which the figure needs because the width
/// that gives a differential impedance depends on it. The router draws the two nets as a diff
/// pair, whatever their names (issue #1025): each at the width that gives the differential
/// impedance at `.spacing`, never nearer its partner (or other copper) than `.spacing`, and
/// beside its partner within a grid cell of it.
interface PairImpedance {
    .p: Pin
    .n: Pin
    /// The differential impedance, as a single value (`90ohm`).
    .impedance: Resistance
    /// The space between the two tracks, edge to edge, as a single value.
    .spacing: Length
    /// The most the two tracks' lengths may differ by (issue #1234), as `DiffPair.skew` is: the
    /// router lengthens the shorter until they differ by no more, and `netsmith board nets` and
    /// `board drc` judge the pair against its top. Offered at `0mm..0.1mm`, the router's target;
    /// a board whose link asks for less narrows it (`.pair.skew &= 0mm..0.05mm`).
    .skew: Length
    .skew ?= 0mm..0.1mm
}

/// Two nets that are a diff pair, with no impedance stated (issue #1193): `.p` and `.n` are
/// joined with `~` to the pair's two nets, and the router routes them as it routes the nets of a
/// `PairImpedance` or two nets whose names differ only in a suffix pair (`dp` and `dm`, `_P` and
/// `_N`): as one object, at one pitch (the gap between them is their clearance), their vias side
/// by side, and the shorter net lengthened by a meander until the two are as long to the router's
/// skew limit, `.skew` (issue #1223; `netsmith board nets` measures it). It sets no width and no spacing:
/// each net keeps the width and the clearance it has. A pair whose impedance matters is a `PairImpedance`, which is a diff
/// pair too. A bus of the standard library whose lines are a pair says so itself (`std::bus::Can`).
///
/// Why an interface of its own, joined with `~`, as `TrackImpedance` and `PairImpedance` are:
/// it adds no syntax, a module carries the pair out through its interface, and the nets are the
/// ones the pins are joined to, not strings that a typo would miss. Built by an agent while the
/// owner was away; the other spellings are in the pull request of issue #1193.
interface DiffPair {
    .p: Pin
    .n: Pin
    /// The most the two nets' track lengths may differ by (issue #1234): the router lengthens
    /// the shorter with a meander until they differ by no more, and `netsmith board nets` and
    /// `board drc` judge the pair against the top of the range. Offered at `0mm..0.1mm`, the
    /// router's grid, which is well inside what the links netsmith's boards carry ask (USB 2.0
    /// high speed asks a whole path under 100 ps, many mm of track); a board whose link asks
    /// for less narrows it (`.lines.skew &= 0mm..0.05mm`), and a bus of the standard library
    /// offers the figure of its standard (`std::bus::Can`). Why a field and not an interface of
    /// its own: the owner's decision on issue #1234.
    .skew: Length
    .skew ?= 0mm..0.1mm
}

/// Nets whose tracks are matched in length (issue #1234), a parallel bus or a byte lane: each
/// line is joined with `~` to a net of the group, `.lane.lines[0] ~ .u.d0` up to
/// `.lane.lines[7] ~ .u.d7`, and the group has as many lines as the indices the board joins (the
/// owner's decision on issue #1252: no count is written anywhere). The router lengthens the
/// shorter nets towards the longest with meanders until no two differ by more than the top of
/// `.tolerance`, and `netsmith board nets` and `board drc` list each net's length and the group's
/// spread. A line on no net (an index skipped), two lines on one net, a group of fewer than two
/// nets and a tolerance with no top or a top of 0 are errors of the build.
///
/// Why an interface joined with `~`, as `DiffPair` is: a module carries the group out through
/// its interface, and the nets are the ones the lines are joined to, not strings that a typo
/// would miss (the owner's decision on issue #1234).
interface LengthGroup {
    /// The group's nets, one line for each, named by index from `[0]`.
    .lines: [Pin]
    /// The most the longest and the shortest net may differ by, as a range whose top is the
    /// limit (`.lane.tolerance &= 0mm..0.5mm`). It has no default: what a bus allows depends on
    /// its clock and its interface's timing, which the board knows and the library does not.
    .tolerance: Length
}
